from assignment_second_half_common import *
qs=[]
qs.append(Q(8,1,1,'UCS graph and termination','Treat the assignment graph as undirected, with edge costs in km: AB=5, AC=2, BD=6, BE=3, CF=4, DE=2, DG=6, EF=1, FG=3. UCS starts at A, retains cheaper paths to frontier nodes, and tests the goal E when it is removed at minimum cumulative cost. Which path and cost are returned?', ['A→C→F→E, 7 km','A→B→E, 8 km','A→B→D→E, 13 km','A→C→F→G→D→E, 17 km'],['The costs are 2+4+1=7, and E is improved from 8 to 7 before it is removed.','This is a valid route and the first E route generated from B, but it is improved before goal removal.','The route is valid but costs 5+6+2=13, not the minimum.','This valid detour costs 2+4+3+6+2=17, exceeding the 7 km route.'],[C(34,'Week 8 item 1, complete weighted graph and path-cost explanation','AB5, AC2, BD6, BE3, CF4, DE2, DG6, EF1, FG3; A→C→F→E costs 7.','figure')],'UCS selects by cumulative cost, not edge count or the first generated goal route.',assumptions=['Undirected graph; all costs positive; no unshown edges; graph-search relaxation; goal test on frontier removal. No relevant cost tie occurs.'],steps=['Remove A(0), generating C(2) and B(5).','Remove C(2), generating F(6).','Remove B(5), generating E(8) and D(11).','Remove F(6), improving E to 7 and generating G(9).','Remove E(7), and return A→C→F→E.'],formulas=[F('g(v)=min(g(v),g(u)+c(u,v)); g(A)=0',{'g':('best cumulative start-to-node path cost','km'),'u':('expanded node','node identifier'),'v':('neighbor node','node identifier'),'c':('edge-cost function','km'),'A':('start node','node identifier')},['Nonnegative edge costs; relax neighbors before selecting the next minimum-cost frontier node.'])],qtype='algorithm_trace',difficulty='medium',angles=['all_graph_edges','cheaper_frontier_update','goal_test_timing','path_cost']))
qs.append(Q(8,2,2,'Heuristic role and UCS limit','In A*, g(n) is accumulated cost and h(n) estimates remaining cost. Which statement correctly describes h and the special case h(n)=0 for every node, using the same frontier and tie rules?', ['h estimates cost-to-go; setting it to zero makes A* use the UCS cost priority.','h measures cost already paid; setting it to zero makes A* use breadth-first depth priority.','h is the complete estimated path cost; setting it to zero makes A* use greedy goal-distance priority.','h is the next edge’s exact cost; setting it to zero makes A* ignore accumulated cost.'],['A* uses f=g+h, so h=0 leaves the UCS priority g.','Cost already paid is g; UCS and breadth-first need not agree with unequal edge costs.','The complete estimate is f, and greedy best-first uses h alone rather than g+h.','A heuristic estimates remaining route cost, not merely the next edge, and g remains in f.'],[C(35,'Week 8 item 2, heuristic cost-to-go option, and item 3, f=g+h explanation','Heuristic estimates cost-to-go; A* expands lowest total estimated cost f(n)=g(n)+h(n).')],'Adding a heuristic guides search without replacing accumulated cost. Optimality is a separate claim requiring appropriate conditions.',formulas=[F('f(n)=g(n)+h(n); h(n)=0 ⇒ f(n)=g(n)',{'f':('frontier priority estimate','cost units'),'g':('cost accumulated from start','cost units'),'h':('estimated remaining cost','cost units'),'n':('frontier node','node identifier')},['Use identical graph-search and tie rules when comparing A* with UCS.'])],angles=['heuristic_definition','UCS_limit','greedy_distinction']))
qs.append(Q(8,3,3,'Three frontier priorities','The complete valid frontier contains A:(g=4.0,h=2.0), B:(g=3.0,h=2.4), C:(g=1.0,h=4.8), all in the same cost units. For one next-node choice, A* minimizes g+h, UCS minimizes g, and greedy best-first minimizes h. Which ordered triple (A*, UCS, greedy) is selected?', ['(B, C, A)','(A, C, B)','(C, B, A)','(B, A, C)'],['The sum minimum is B=5.4, g minimum is C=1.0, and h minimum is A=2.0.','A has the lowest h rather than lowest sum; B does not have the lowest h.','C has the lowest g, while B has the lowest g+h; this swaps A* and UCS.','The A* result is right, but UCS does not choose the largest g and greedy does not choose the largest h.'],[C(35,'Week 8 item 3, all candidate g/h rows and f calculations','A:g4,h2; B:g3,h2.4; C:g1,h4.8. f(A)=6.0, f(B)=5.4, f(C)=5.8.','equation')],'Different priority functions can choose different nodes from exactly the same frontier.',assumptions=['No other frontier entries, stale records or ties; the supplied priority rules define the three comparisons.'],steps=['Compute sums: A=6.0, B=5.4, C=5.8, hence A* selects B.','Compare g alone: 1.0 at C is minimum.','Compare h alone: 2.0 at A is minimum.'],formulas=[F('f(n)=g(n)+h(n); n_A*=argmin f; n_UCS=argmin g; n_greedy=argmin h',{'f':('A* frontier priority','cost units'),'g':('accumulated path cost','cost units'),'h':('remaining-cost estimate','cost units'),'n':('candidate node','node identifier'),'n_A*':('A* next node','node identifier'),'n_UCS':('UCS next node','node identifier'),'n_greedy':('greedy next node','node identifier')},['Complete valid frontier; unique minima.'])],qtype='numerical',difficulty='medium',angles=['Astar_working','frontier_validity','UCS_greedy_contrast']))
qs.append(Q(8,4,4,'Bug0 recurrence trace','A deterministic Bug0 run has the following supplied event trace in a static environment: at obstacle hit H it enters boundary following; at L the goal heading becomes locally unblocked, so it leaves; the goal-directed segment returns it to the same hit state H. Sensor readings and the selected boundary-following direction then repeat. There is no stored-hit or loop-detection rule. What follows?', ['The same H→L→H cycle can repeat; local leave eligibility alone does not establish progress to the goal.','Returning to H automatically reports the goal unreachable, as part of the stated Bug0 policy.','The second visit automatically triggers a full-perimeter search for the globally closest leave point.','The second visit automatically reverses the boundary-following direction and prevents another cycle.'],['The same state and observations under a deterministic unchanged policy reproduce the same actions.','That requires a remembered-hit termination test not included in this policy.','That adds a Bug1-like full-boundary procedure absent from the supplied leave rule.','The direction is stipulated to repeat; automatic reversal would be an additional policy.'],[C(36,'Week 8 item 4, Bug0 move/leave rule and looping explanation','Move toward the goal, follow obstacle boundary until goal heading is unblocked; no memory of past positions can cause loops around non-convex/U-shaped obstacles.')],'A locally clear goal heading is not a certificate of global progress. The supplied recurrence demonstrates the failure without claiming every concave obstacle produces it.',assumptions=['The supplied event transitions are feasible for this example; static environment, unchanged goal and deterministic control; no extra loop detection.'],steps=['At H, the rule selects boundary following.','At L, the local visibility rule selects goal-directed motion.','The supplied motion returns to the identical H state.','With identical inputs and no additional memory rule, the H→L→H sequence repeats.'],qtype='algorithm_trace',angles=['actual_event_trace','local_vs_global_progress','Bug1_memory_contrast']))
qs.append(Q(8,5,5,'DQN stabilization mechanisms','A DQN learner’s bootstrap labels change whenever its online value estimates change. Which design choice most directly slows that particular moving-target feedback?', ['Compute bootstrap values with a separate network whose parameters are held or updated more slowly.','Shuffle stored transitions into replay minibatches while keeping bootstrap and online parameters identical at every update.','Increase ε-greedy exploration while keeping bootstrap and online parameters identical at every update.','Use a separate action-selection estimate to reduce maximization bias, without specifying any slower target-parameter schedule.'],['A delayed or slower target parameter set reduces immediate coupling between each online update and its label.','Replay reduces sample correlation but does not by itself delay changes in identical target parameters.','Exploration changes action sampling, not the immediate target-network coupling.','Decoupling selection and evaluation addresses a different bias; it does not by itself state the slower target update asked for.'],[C(36,'Week 8 item 5, main/target-network motivation','Separate target network φ alongside main network θ to prevent instability from chasing a moving target.')],'The target network reduces one source of instability; it is not a convergence guarantee or a replacement for replay and exploration.',assumptions=['Compare mechanisms by their direct effect on bootstrap-label coupling, not overall possible training benefit.'],angles=['moving_target_mechanism','replay_vs_target','no_convergence_guarantee']))
qs.append(Q(8,6,6,'DQN target and terminal mask','The online estimate Qθ(s,Right)=4.3, reward r=1.0, discount γ=0.9, and next-state target-network values are 3.0 and 5.0. For a nonterminal transition, let y=r+γ max Qφ(s′,a′) and δ=y−Qθ(s,Right). If the same transition were terminal, use no bootstrap term. Which tuple (nonterminal y, nonterminal δ, terminal y) is correct?', ['(5.5, 1.2, 1.0)','(4.87, 0.57, 1.0)','(5.5, −1.2, 1.0)','(5.5, 1.2, 5.5)'],['The max is 5; target 1+0.9×5=5.5, residual 5.5−4.3=1.2, terminal target 1.0.','4.87 uses the current online value 4.3 in place of the next-state maximum 5.0.','The target is correct, but the residual sign contradicts δ=y−Q.','The nonterminal values are right, but terminal transitions do not bootstrap under the stated rule.'],[C(36,'Week 8 item 6, current prediction and reward, continuing onto page 37','Q(s,Right)=4.3; r=1.0; transition to s′.','equation'),C(37,'Week 8 item 6 continuation, target values, discount and worked target','Q(s′,Left;φ)=3.0; Q(s′,Right;φ)=5.0; γ=0.9; y=1+0.9×5=5.5. Numeric 5.5 and stray Answer:C are both printed.','equation')],'The source target is the explicitly nonterminal case. The adaptation adds a terminal-mask comparison and defines the residual sign; the original stray letter is not an MCQ key.',assumptions=['Ordinary one-step DQN max target, not Double DQN; terminal means zero remaining bootstrap value; all Q/reward values use the same reward scale.'],steps=['Choose max(3.0,5.0)=5.0 from the next-state target network.','Nonterminal target y=1+0.9×5=5.5.','δ=5.5−4.3=1.2.','For terminal d=1, y=r=1.0.'],formulas=[F('y=r+γ(1−d)max_a′ Qφ(s′,a′); δ=y−Qθ(s,a)',{'y':('one-step learning target','reward units'),'r':('immediate reward','reward units'),'γ':('discount factor','dimensionless'),'d':('terminal indicator, 1 terminal and 0 otherwise','dimensionless'),'Qφ':('target-network action-value estimate','reward units'),'Qθ':('online action-value estimate','reward units'),'s':('current state','state identifier'),'s′':('next state','state identifier'),'a':('selected current action','action identifier'),'a′':('candidate next action','action identifier'),'δ':('target-minus-prediction TD residual','reward units')},['No bootstrap at a terminal transition.'])],qtype='numerical',difficulty='medium',angles=['target_working','current_vs_next','TD_sign','terminal_boundary']))
qs.append(Q(8,7,7,'Waypoint feedback versus preprocessing','A GNSS route has already been expressed in a consistent local coordinate frame and its waypoints selected. Which continuing operation constitutes waypoint tracking rather than route preprocessing?', ['Repeatedly compare live robot position with the current waypoint and update motion commands from the error.','Convert the stored route coordinates once and then send the original command sequence without position feedback.','Recompute the road graph’s edge weights, but do not compare the current robot position with the active waypoint.','Estimate satellite geometry quality, but do not use the position-to-waypoint error in control.'],['This closes the position feedback loop around the current waypoint.','Coordinate conversion is useful preprocessing but the proposed execution is open-loop.','Graph costs support planning; omitting the live tracking error does not close the waypoint loop.','GNSS quality assessment is not itself feedback control toward a waypoint.'],[C(37,'Week 8 item 7, waypoint-tracking purpose','Close the control loop by continuously comparing live GNSS positions against current target waypoints.')],'Planning where to go and converting coordinates do not replace feedback on where the robot currently is.',assumptions=['Coordinate frames are already consistent; no new GNSS measurement or physical execution is claimed.'],angles=['tracking_feedback','preprocessing_distinction']))
qs.append(Q(8,8,8,'Pseudorange clock offset','After satellite-clock correction, use the simplified model ρ=R+cδt. Here δt is a common receiver clock offset, c=3.0×10^8 m/s, and δt=+2 μs. Which interpretation and range contribution are correct?', ['A receiver timing offset contributes +600 m to pseudorange.','A receiver timing offset contributes +0.6 m to pseudorange.','An inter-constellation frame rotation contributes +600 m to pseudorange.','A receiver timing offset contributes −600 m to pseudorange.'],['2 μs=2×10^−6 s, so the stated plus-sign model adds 600 m.','0.6 m would correspond to 2 ns at the supplied light speed, not 2 μs.','The cδt term represents timing bias here, not a coordinate-frame rotation.','The magnitude is correct but the sign contradicts ρ=R+cδt and positive δt.'],[C(37,'Week 8 item 8, clock-bias term and highlighted timing discrepancy','Clock-bias term cδt; discrepancy between satellite atomic clock and receiver low-power clock.','equation'),C(38,'Top continuation of Week 8 item 8, printed key and reference','Answer:B; Lecture39 GPS Path Finding slide9.')],'Timing error is converted to a length bias by multiplying by signal speed. The numerical offset and sign convention are supplied in this adaptation.',assumptions=['Single corrected time system; satellite clock and other propagation errors omitted from this simplified example.'],steps=['Convert 2 μs to 2×10^−6 s.','cδt=(3×10^8)(2×10^−6)=600 m.','The model adds this positive receiver-clock term to geometric range.'],formulas=[F('ρ=R+cδt; b=cδt',{'ρ':('measured pseudorange in simplified model','m'),'R':('geometric satellite–receiver range','m'),'c':('supplied signal speed','m/s'),'δt':('receiver clock offset after satellite corrections','s'),'b':('range bias due to clock offset','m')},['Positive δt produces positive range bias under the defined sign convention.'])],qtype='numerical',angles=['clock_bias_meaning','time_length_units','sign_convention']))
qs.append(Q(8,9,9,'Singleton weighted output','A fuzzy cooling controller has two output singletons: Slow at 20% power with weight 0.7 and Medium at 60% with weight 0.3. Explicitly use z=(Σμᵢzᵢ)/(Σμᵢ), with a plus between the two denominator weights. What crisp power results?', ['32%','40%','48%','14%'],['(0.7×20+0.3×60)/(0.7+0.3)=(14+18)/1=32%.','40% is the unweighted midpoint of 20% and 60%.','48% swaps the weights: 0.3×20+0.7×60=48.','14% keeps only the weighted Slow contribution and drops Medium.'],[C(38,'Week 8 item 9, singleton weights, centers and malformed denominator','Weights 0.7 at 20% and 0.3 at 60%; answer32. Printed denominator reads 0.7_0.3 without a plus. A stray Answer:C and Lecture39 Fuzzy Logic Control reference are also printed.','equation','conflict')],'The explicit normalized singleton formula yields 32%. The malformed denominator is not silently copied into executable mathematics; original stray key/reference text stays in its audit record.',assumptions=['Singleton centers and nonnegative weights; positive weight sum; no continuous membership-shape integration is being requested.'],steps=['Weighted contributions: 14 and 18 percentage points.','Sum of weights: 0.7+0.3=1.0.','Divide weighted sum 32 by 1.0, yielding 32%.'],formulas=[F('z=(Σᵢ μᵢ zᵢ)/(Σᵢ μᵢ)',{'z':('crisp singleton weighted-average output','% power'),'μᵢ':('nonnegative firing/output weight of singleton i','dimensionless'),'zᵢ':('singleton power center','% power'),'i':('singleton index','dimensionless')},['Sum of weights strictly positive; use a discrete weighted average, not an arbitrary continuous centroid.'])],qtype='numerical',conflict={'issue':'The printed denominator lacks the addition operator; numeric answer32 and stray letter C coexist.','resolution':'The adaptation explicitly supplies the normalized weighted-sum denominator and preserves original key/reference defects outside the study pool.','method':'Evaluate the supplied singleton formula and confirm its numerator and positive weight sum separately.'},angles=['singleton_weighting','normalization','printed_equation_defect']))
qs.append(Q(8,10,10,'Crisp value versus fuzzy membership','After defuzzification, a controller issues the single command 32% power. Which statement accurately describes this crisp output value?', ['It is one definite command value; its numerical value may still change at the next control update.','It is a membership degree of 0.32 in every fuzzy output set.','It must stay fixed over time because crisp means constant.','It means every crisp set in fuzzy logic must contain exactly one element.'],['Crisp describes a definite value at this update, not time invariance or a universal claim about sets.','32% is the physical command scale; membership in a set requires that set’s specified membership function.','A deterministic scalar output can change with new inputs while remaining crisp.','The question concerns a crisp value; crisp sets may contain multiple elements with binary membership.'],[C(38,'Week 8 item 10, highlighted definition of a crisp value','A clearly defined, measurable value, also known as a singleton value.')],'The source’s singleton wording is scoped to one output value. It does not imply that all crisp sets are singleton sets.',assumptions=['Discuss a single numerical control command, not the cardinality of an arbitrary crisp set.'],angles=['crisp_value','time_variation','value_vs_membership','set_scope']))
notes=[(34,'1','Viewed every graph node/edge and weight: AB5,AC2,BD6,BE3,CF4,DE2,DG6,EF1,FG3; no arrows. Read all four routes and costs; G is not goal E.'),(35,'2','Viewed Q2 heuristic cost-to-go key and alternatives; Q3 all g/h rows and f sums 6.0,5.4,5.8, with B highlighted.'),(36,'3','Viewed Bug0 movement/leave rule, loop explanation and four alternatives; DQN target/main-network motivation and alternatives; beginning of numerical target Q6. No actual obstacle drawing appears.'),(37,'4','Viewed Q6 remaining numbers, numeric5.5 plus stray Answer:C and Bellman working; waypoint-tracking explanation; clock-bias options and highlighted clock discrepancy.'),(38,'5','Viewed Q8 continuation answer/reference; Q9 singleton weights, malformed 0.7_0.3 denominator, numeric32 and stray Answer:C, mismatched Lecture39 Fuzzy label; Q10 crisp-value wording and END.')]
